{"team":"TB","col":"#FF5A4D","identity":"balanced","funnel":"pass-funnel","plays_pg":62.7,"adot":7.5,"report_html":"<div class=\"report\"><h2>A Balanced Attack</h2><p>There's no bell-cow to bank on \u2014 the lead back sees just <strong>47%</strong> of carries (league ~59%), a committee that caps any one back's ceiling and makes their rushing props matchup- and script-dependent.</p><h3>The Offensive Blueprint</h3><p>When they fall behind they abandon the run entirely \u2014 58% pass while trailing, so their pass-catchers have huge comeback-script ceilings.</p><p>By the numbers it's an efficient unit \u2014 a 46% success rate (above the 44% average).</p><h3>In the Red Zone</h3><p>Their red-zone identity is <strong>pass-first</strong> (55% pass), which puts scoring equity on <strong>Chris Godwin Jr.</strong>.</p><h2>Defense \u2014 They Force the Throw</h2><p>They surrender <strong>99 rushing</strong> and <strong>259 passing</strong> yards per game (league ~120 / ~230). They wall the run and force the throw \u2014 <strong>attack with the opposing WR room</strong>.</p><p><strong>Soft against backs out of the backfield</strong> \u2014 27 rec yds/game (vs ~18). A rec-yards target.</p><p><strong>Soft against opposing tight ends</strong> \u2014 40 rec yds/game (vs ~35). A rec-yards target.</p><p>They lock down opposing WR1s \u2014 just 52 rec yds/game (vs ~58); fade receiving props there.</p><p>They leak <strong>TE touchdowns</strong> (0.43/game vs ~0.24) \u2014 an anytime-TD spot to attack.</p><h2>The Betting Angles</h2><p>Bottom line: their pass-catchers have massive ceilings in comeback scripts (fade-material as underdogs); <strong>opposing WR1/WR2</strong> are the target \u2014 this D forces the throw; <strong>opposing TE anytime-TD</strong> is a repeatable angle.</p><p><em>Built on 2024\u201325 data; the profile rebuilds through the season as the current staff and roster set their own tendencies.</em></p></div>","depth":{"QB":[{"name":"Baker Mayfield","slot":"QB1","sta